.TH SYS_TIME 2
.SH NAME
sys_time \- the wall clock (SYS_TIME)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include "mk.h"

int64_t sys_time(int64_t set);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sys_time
with
.I set
0 returns the date and time: nanoseconds since 1970-01-01 00:00 UTC. It
is the real-time clock (the battery-backed clock chip), read once at
boot before any program runs, plus the time since boot
.RB ( sys_clock (2)).
.PP
With
.I set
not 0, root sets the clock to that many nanoseconds since 1970; the time
since boot keeps counting from there. The clock chip itself is not written.
.PP
.B date_str
(mk.h) turns the value into "YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM".
.SH RETURN VALUE
Nanoseconds since 1970 (after setting, the new time), or a negative error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EPERM
.I set
is not 0 and the caller is not root.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sys_clock (2),
.BR stat (1)
